      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the possible degrees of the remainder when a polynomial \\( P(x) \\) is divided by \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\), we need to use the polynomial long division method or the concept of the division algorithm for polynomials. The division algorithm states that for any polynomials \\( P(x) \\) and \\( D(x) \\) (where \\( D(x) \\neq 0 \\)), there exist unique polynomials \\( Q(x) \\) (the quotient) and \\( R(x) \\) (the remainder) such that:\n\n\\[ P(x) = D(x) Q(x) + R(x) \\]\n\nwhere the degree of \\( R(x) \\) is less than the degree of \\( D(x) \\).\n\nGiven \\( D(x) = 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\), the degree of \\( D(x) \\) is 2. Therefore, the degree of the remainder \\( R(x) \\) must be less than 2. This means the possible degrees of \\( R(x) \\) are 0 and 1.\n\nLet's verify this step-by-step:\n\n1. ** polynomial degree comparison**:\n   - The degree of \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\) is 2 (since the term of highest degree is \\( 2x^2 \\)).\n   - The polynomial \\( P(x) \\) has an indeterminate degree, but it must be expressed in such a way that the division by \\( D(x) \\) results in another polynomial or a constant remainder.\n\n2. ** possible remainders**:\n   - If \\( P(x) \\) is divided by \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\), the remainder \\( R(x) \\) must be a polynomial of degree less than 2.\n   - A polynomial of degree less than 2 can be written in the form \\( R(x) = ax + b \\) where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are constants.\n\n3. ** conclusion**:\n   - The possible degrees of the remainder \\( R(x) \\) are 0 (constant remainder) and 1 (linear remainder).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0, 1}\n\\]",
